EXT. WATER TOWER ON THE PLAINS - DAWN

A rusted tank on stilts against an empty sky. A rope ladder swings.

He climbs it, slow, rifle across his back. At the top he kneels, takes off his hat, and peers over the rim. She is already there, sitting cross-legged, sipping from a tin cup.

She doesn't look at him.

GASPAR REYES
Morning.

ELLIE VANCE
Morning.

GASPAR REYES
You waiting on me?

ELLIE VANCE
Waiting on weather.

He settles beside her. She offers the cup. He drinks and hands it back.

GASPAR REYES
They say the bank's closed.

ELLIE VANCE
They say a lot.

GASPAR REYES
Is it?

She shrugs. Far below, a dust cloud rises on the road. Both watch it come.

GASPAR REYES (CONT'D)
That's a posse.

ELLIE VANCE
That's a wagon.

GASPAR REYES
You sure?

ELLIE VANCE
No.

He reaches for the rifle. She puts a hand on his wrist. The dust draws closer, a hundred yards, fifty. A mule brays.

ELLIE VANCE (CONT'D)
It's a wagon.

He lets go of the rifle. She drinks the last of the tea.
